---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router8
diagnosis_text:
The OSPF adjacency between router7 and router8 failed because the two
routers are advertising the shared 172.25.1.0/24 link segment into
different OSPF areas. Router7 (eth5, 172.25.1.1/24) places this link
in Area 0 (`network 172.25.1.0/24 area 0`), while router8 (eth4,
172.25.1.2/24) places it in Area 7 (`network 172.25.1.0/24 area 7`).

In OSPF, the Area ID is carried in every Hello packet. When two routers
on the same segment advertise different area IDs, their Hello packets are
rejected at the parameter-mismatch check and the state machine never
advances past INIT — exactly the symptom reported. Both interfaces are
physically UP with matching MTU (1500 B), Hello/Dead timers (10/40 s),
and correct IP addressing (same /24), so the physical and timer layers
are healthy.

The fault is isolated to router8's OSPF process. The recent "configuration
change on router8" that preceded the symptom report modified the network
statement for 172.25.1.0/24 from area 0 to area 7. Campus-1-to-Campus-1
traffic was unaffected because those paths use the 192.168.10.0/24,
172.30.1.0/24, and 10.0.0.0/24 adjacencies (all still Full), while Campus 2
routes that traverse the router7↔router8 link flapped as the adjacency fell.

Root cause: router8's OSPF `network 172.25.1.0/24 area 7` statement
incorrectly assigns the inter-router link to a non-backbone area while
router7 expects area 0.

Corrective fix: On router8, in `router ospf`, replace
`network 172.25.1.0/24 area 7` with `network 172.25.1.0/24 area 0`.
This will realign both endpoints to Area 0 and the adjacency will
re-establish within one Hello interval.
=== END ===
```